Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Adapted for the Screen
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

The sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley. BUCK, a massive one-hundred-and-forty-pound dog--half St. Bernard, half Scotch shepherd, with a thick furry coat and broad chest--lies regally on a manicured lawn. 

(SOUND: A gentle breeze rustles the tall poplars)

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

MANUEL, a gardener's helper in worn overalls, approaches. He pats Buck's head, then slips a stout rope around Buck's neck.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Because men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal. These men wanted dogs, and the dogs they wanted were heavy dogs, with strong muscles by which to toil.

Manuel tightens the rope. Buck growls, confused, as Manuel drags him toward the orchard.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

A high-walled yard. Buck is trapped inside a wooden crate, his eyes bloodshot, mouth foaming. 

(SOUND: Wood splintering under heavy blows)

The MAN IN RED SWEATER, stout and grim, drives a hatchet into the crate, prying the wood apart. He drops the hatchet and shifts a heavy wooden club to his right hand.

Buck launches his one hundred and forty pounds of fury straight at the man. 

In mid-air, a shocking blow from the club smashes Buck to the ground. 

(SOUND: A sickening thud of wood against bone)

Buck snarls, a sound part bark and more scream, and leaps again. Again, the club brings him crushingly down. Blood flows from his nose and mouth, flecking his beautiful coat.

For the last time, Buck rushes. The man strikes a shrewd blow. Buck crumples, knocked utterly senseless.

Buck's eyes flutter open. He lies where he fell, watching the man.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

The man fearlessly pats Buck's battered head. Buck's hair involuntarily bristles, but he endures it.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
He was beaten; but he was not broken. He saw, once for all, that he stood no chance against a man with a club. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

A chaotic camp of tents and sleds. White mushy stuff falls through the air. Buck sniffs it, licks it. It bites like fire, then vanishes. 

(SOUND: The howling of wind and distant, savage dog fights)

FRANCOIS, a swarthy French-Canadian half-breed, fastens an arrangement of straps and buckles onto Buck. PERRAULT, a weazened French-Canadian, watches approvingly.

Nearby stands SPITZ, a big, snow-white dog from Spitzbergen. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue in a way he has of laughing, his eyes cold.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
He had been suddenly jerked from the heart of civilization and flung into the heart of things primordial. Here was neither peace, nor rest. They were savages, all of them, who knew no law but the law of club and fang.

Francois cracks a whip. Buck leans into the harness.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

A frozen, blinding white expanse. Buck toils in the traces, pulling a heavy sled. Spitz is the lead-dog, running ahead. 

(SOUND: The rhythmic crunch of paws and sled runners on packed snow)

Spitz cunningly throws his weight in the traces, jerking Buck off balance. Buck stumbles. Spitz growls a sharp reproof, baring his teeth.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Spitz never lost an opportunity of showing his teeth. He even went out of his way to bully Buck, striving constantly to start the fight which could end only in the death of one or the other.

Buck recovers his footing, his eyes fixed on the white dog ahead.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Over the whiteness and silence broods a ghostly calm. The visible breaths of fifty sled dogs rise slowly in the frosty air, forming an expectant circle.

(SOUND: The low, collective panting of the wolfish circle)

In the center, Buck and Spitz circle each other, ears laid back, snarling. Buck is streaming with blood, panting hard. Spitz is untouched, his white coat gleaming in the moonlight.

Spitz rushes. Fang clashes fang. Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and leaps lightly away.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Spitz was a practised fighter. In vain Buck strove to sink his teeth in the neck of the big white dog. But Buck possessed a quality that made for greatness--imagination.

Buck rushes, as though attempting a shoulder trick, but at the last instant sweeps low to the snow. His teeth close on Spitz's left fore leg. 

(SOUND: A loud crunch of breaking bone)

Spitz faces him on three legs, struggling madly. Buck repeats the trick, breaking the right fore leg. 

Spitz staggers back and forth, snarling with horrible menace. The dark circle of dogs tightens. Buck springs in, shoulder squarely meeting shoulder. Spitz disappears under the silvery breaths.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. SKAGUAY TRAIL - DAY

Springtime. The trail is soft and rotting. Buck staggers at the head of a long, ragged team. He is a perambulating skeleton. His hair hangs limp and draggled, matted with dried blood. His muscles are wasted to knotty strings.

(SOUND: The heavy, dragging scrape of a top-heavy sled)

HAL, a youngster of nineteen with a big Colt's revolver and a hunting knife strapped to his belt, swings a whip savagely. 

MERCEDES, pretty but worn, sits atop the overloaded sled, weeping.

HAL
Mush! Mush on there!

The whip bites into Buck. He drops down in the traces like a dead dog. The spark of life flutters feebly. He totters to his feet and staggers on.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In less than five months they had travelled twenty-five hundred miles. They were not half living, or quarter living. They were simply so many bags of bones in which sparks of life fluttered faintly.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

The sled halts by the river bank. The dogs drop down as though struck dead. 

JOHN THORNTON, a large man with stringy, knotty muscles, sits on a log, whittling an axe-handle. He watches the ruined team.

(SOUND: The ominous, deep throbbing of the river ice straining to break loose)

HAL
They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I tell you straight, I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Hal uncoils his whip. The lash flashes out. The other dogs make painful efforts to rise. Buck lies quietly where he has fallen. 

Hal exchanges the whip for a heavy club. He rains blows upon Buck. Buck feels strangely numb, the sensations of pain leaving him.

Suddenly, John Thornton springs forward, uttering an inarticulate cry. He strikes Hal, hurling him backward. 

(SOUND: A heavy thud as Hal hits the ground)

Thornton stands over Buck, his chest heaving, too convulsed with rage to speak.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Hal draws his long hunting-knife. Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the axe-handle, knocking the knife to the ground. Thornton stoops, picks it up, and with two strokes cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, defeated, guides the sled away with the remaining dogs. Mercedes rides on top. 

Thornton kneels beside Buck, his rough, kindly hands searching for broken bones. 

A quarter of a mile away, the back end of the sled drops down. 

(SOUND: A sharp crack of ice, followed by a piercing scream from Mercedes)

A whole section of ice gives way. Dogs and humans disappear into a yawning hole. The bottom has dropped out of the trail.

John Thornton and Buck look at each other.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck weakly licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. WILDERNESS CAMP - DAY

Summer. A broad valley where gold shows like yellow butter in the washing-pan. Buck, his muscles swelled out and his glorious furry coat restored, lies at John Thornton's feet.

(SOUND: The gentle trickle of a stream and the hum of forest insects)

Buck looks up into Thornton's face, studying it with eager adoration. Thornton reaches down, taking Buck's head roughly between his hands, shaking him back and forth.

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck springs to his feet, his mouth laughing, his eyes eloquent, his throat vibrant with unuttered sound. He seizes Thornton's hand in his mouth, closing fiercely but leaving no mark.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. But in spite of this great love he bore John Thornton, the strain of the primitive remained alive and active.

Buck turns his head, looking past Thornton, deep into the green shade of the forest.

EXT. DEEP FOREST - NIGHT

The dim twilight of a summer midnight. Buck stands motionless among the trees, his nostrils quivering, his mane bristling in recurrent waves.

(SOUND: A long-drawn howl, distinct and definite, echoes through the timber)

Buck stalks into an open clearing. Erect on its haunches, with nose pointed to the sky, sits a long, lean TIMBER WOLF.

The wolf ceases howling, sensing Buck. It darts away. Buck follows in wild leapings. 

They run side by side through the sombre twilight, straight up the creek bed. Buck is wildly glad.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
He was at last answering the call, running by the side of his wood brother. He had done this thing before, somewhere in that other and dimly remembered world, and he was doing it again.

Buck stops by a running stream. He remembers John Thornton. He sits down, turns about, and starts slowly on the back track. The wild brother howls mournfully as Buck leaves.

EXT. WILDERNESS CAMP - DAY

Buck drops down into the valley toward camp. The silence of the forest is pregnant. The bird life has flitted. 

(SOUND: A faint, sing-song chant of many voices rises and falls)

Buck belies forward to the edge of the clearing. 

The spruce-bough lodge is wrecked. The YEEHAT CHIEF and a dozen indigenous hunters, painted and armed with spears, dance about the ruins. 

Near the edge of a deep pool, John Thornton lies dead in the muddy water.

A gust of overpowering rage sweeps over Buck. He growls aloud with a terrible ferocity. 

Buck hurls himself into the clearing, a live hurricane of fury. He springs at the Yeehat Chief.

(SOUND: The tearing of flesh and a fountain of spouting blood)

Buck rips the Chief's throat wide open. He bounds to the next man, tearing wide his throat. He plunges about in their very midst, destroying in constant and terrific motion. 

Arrows whiz past him. A panic seizes the Yeehats. They flee in terror to the woods.

Buck stands alone in the desolated camp. He walks to the pool and sniffs the water. He broods, a great void aching within him.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

A full moon rises high over the trees, bathing the land in ghostly day. Buck stands in the center of the clearing, motionless as a statue.

(SOUND: A chorus of sharp yelps grows closer and louder)

Into the clearing pours a silvery flood of wolves. They pause, awed by Buck's size. The boldest one leaps straight for him. Like a flash Buck strikes, breaking its neck. 

Three others try it. Buck pivots, snapping and gashing, driving them back.

The long, lean Timber Wolf advances cautiously, whining softly. Buck whines, and they touch noses. 

An old, battle-scarred wolf comes forward, sits down, points his nose at the moon, and howls. 

(MUSIC: A sweeping, primal orchestral swell builds in the night air)

The call comes to Buck in unmistakable accents. He sits down and howls. 

The pack crowds around him, sniffing in half-friendly manner. The leaders spring away into the woods. Buck runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack. When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
